| Demo | 1 [A1] | happy-path purchase plus one rejected prompt | attack-card deck vs a stock naive wallet, two shooters, live scoreboard, explorer links for execution-phase failures | F12, F14; [X1] |
| UX / product | 1 [A1] | approval page shows the agent's own words | plain-words policy line, approval card built only from decoded calldata and simulated outflow, agent text struck through, one-tap revoke, stamina bar | F8, F15, F18; [E26][E40] |
| Code quality | 1 [A1] | unit tests on a direct EntryPoint call | one `AgentPolicy` contract with a 7579-shaped interface, invariant + fuzz tests, one bundler compatibility run, Slither, threat-model table in README | F4; [E32][E31] |
| Novelty / presentation | 1 [A1] | "we have session keys and caps" | the 1271 side door shown live, and caps on the measured effect; honest bounded-loss claim | F10, F13; [E7][E46] |

## 4. Differentiators

### D1. One door (ERC-1271 refuses the agent key, except a pre-paid x402 envelope)

- **What.** `isValidSignature` returns `0xffffffff` for any hash the agent key signs. That covers EIP-2612, Permit2, marketplace orders and logins. The single exception is an envelope `(MODE_X402, to, value, validAfter, validBefore, nonce, agentSig)`. The account rebuilds the USDC EIP-3009 `TransferWithAuthorization` digest with `from = address(this)` and requires it to equal the hash. It then requires a voucher `(to, value, validBefore, nonce)` that a policy-checked UserOp registered earlier. That UserOp already passed the recipient, per-tx and token-bucket checks and debited the budget by `value`. `isValidSignature` is a view function and cannot debit, so the debit has to happen at registration. An unused voucher is credited back by `reclaim(nonce)` after `validBefore`, if USDC reports the authorization unused. The owner key keeps general 1271.
- **Why it beats the default.** The default entry polices UserOps only. x402 "exact" settles with an off-chain signature and no EntryPoint [E20]. USDC and Permit2 route contract signers to `isValidSignature` [E21][E22][E42]. So a leaked agent key spends with zero UserOps, which breaks [P3] outright.
- **Evidence.** x402 docs define a "permissive" 4337 account as one that accepts a raw controlling-key signature in `isValidSignature` [E42]. ERC-7598 standardises contract signatures for signed transfers [E43].
- **30-second proof.** Card "x402 side door". The leaked key signs a `transferWithAuthorization` to the attacker. The naive wallet's USDC balance drops with no UserOp. On ours the same call reverts with "invalid signature", and an in-policy x402 payment to an allowed API seller still goes through [X2].
- **Closest existing thing.** Smart Sessions lists ERC-1271 signature policies per session [E7]. Its README shows no policy that decodes EIP-3009, charges the shared budget, needs a UserOp-registered nonce, or refuses everything else. We read the README, not the source, so we say "not shown", never "first".

### D2. Meter the effect, sign the outcome

- **What.** In execution (`executeUserOp`, the only phase that may read time [E2]) the account snapshots the native balance, its EntryPoint deposit, tracked ERC-20 balances and allowances to known spenders, Permit2 included. It runs the call and takes the positive outflow and allowance-growth deltas. It reverts if they break the per-tx cap or underflow the per-asset token bucket. For out-of-policy requests the owner signs EIP-712 `(account, chainId, nonce, callDataHash, maxOutflow[asset], validUntil)`. The same post-check enforces that signed bound.
- **Why it beats the default.** Calldata decoding only covers functions we know. An allowed merchant's `buy()` or a router can pull more than the decoded amount, and an `approve` can launder the cap (T3). A measured delta counts what left. Approval text written by the agent is attacker-controlled (T11), and misleading signing requests drove most signature phishing losses [E26].
- **Evidence.** PACE binds a signed record to the execution bytes and reports a 0.00 vs 0.80 unsafe-execution rate in a deterministic sandbox [E46]. It trusts an off-chain simulation. We measure on-chain, so a wrong simulation can only cause a revert, never an overspend.
- **30-second proof.** Card "greedy merchant". The allowed shop's `buy(1 ticket)` secretly calls `transferFrom` for 10x the price. The naive wallet pays it. Ours reverts in execution, so the tx lands with `success=false` and an explorer link. Then the approval card reads "−40 USDC to 0xabc (not in your list), once, until Friday", with the agent's text struck through. One tap, and it executes [X1].
- **Closest existing thing.** Post-execution hooks are established: Safe `checkAfterExecution` [E45], and Uniswap `afterExecute` lists spending limits as a use [E44]. Neither shows a delta-metered cap that also counts allowance growth. PACE is the closest on signing the outcome [E46].

